entry.summary:
When building Flutter applications with Clean Architecture, one pattern that dramatically improves code clarity is Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS). In this article, I'll show you how I implement CQRS in a real Flutter project. CQRS separates read operations (Queries) from write operations (Commands):
Type Purpose Side Effects
Command Mutate state (create, update, delete) Yes
Query Read data No
This separation makes your code: ✅ Easier to test (queries are always idempotent) ✅ Clearer intent (naming says what it does) ✅ Simpler to maintain (each class has one responsibility) Here are the base classes I use: /// Base class for commands that mutate state abstract class Command<Params, Output> { const Command();
FutureResult<Output> call(Params params); }
/// …
